WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.121
Embedded Rust security is a layered
engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.201 --> 00:00:06.451
Start with assets, protected functions,
interfaces, trust boundaries,

3
00:00:06.531 --> 00:00:09.204
physical access assumptions, and lifecycle
requirements.

4
00:00:09.284 --> 00:00:12.377
The device is not only code; it is
hardware, firmware, manufacturing,

5
00:00:12.457 --> 00:00:14.239
updates, service, and field operation.

6
00:00:14.319 --> 00:00:16.416
Use safe Rust abstractions wherever
possible.

7
00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:19.431
When hardware access or platform
integration requires `unsafe`,

8
00:00:19.511 --> 00:00:21.608
keep it small, justified, documented,
tested,

9
00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.309
and wrapped in safe APIs that protect
hardware invariants.

10
00:00:24.389 --> 00:00:27.848
Review register assumptions,
initialization order, peripheral ownership,

11
00:00:27.928 --> 00:00:29.920
interrupts, and platform changes over
time.

12
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.307
Design for bounded memory and predictable
failure.

13
00:00:32.387 --> 00:00:35.427
Review stack, heap, static storage,
buffers, integer conversions,

14
00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:38.809
allocation assumptions, queues, timeouts,
retries, watchdog behavior,

15
00:00:38.889 --> 00:00:39.885
and worst-case input.

16
00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:41.904
Safe Rust reduces memory-corruption risk,

17
00:00:41.984 --> 00:00:45.077
but availability and resource safety still
require explicit limits.

18
00:00:45.157 --> 00:00:48.722
Protect concurrent and interrupt-driven
behavior with clear ownership rules.

19
00:00:48.802 --> 00:00:50.479
Define how shared state is protected,

20
00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:54.071
when DMA buffers are owned by hardware or
software, how completion is signaled,

21
00:00:54.151 --> 00:00:56.405
and how cleanup works after cancellation
or error.

22
00:00:56.485 --> 00:00:58.634
Make timing assumptions visible enough to
test.

23
00:00:58.714 --> 00:01:01.282
Validate all external inputs through
defensive parsing,

24
00:01:01.362 --> 00:01:04.455
protect secrets and device identity, and
treat boot, updates, debug,

25
00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:08.152
dependencies, builds, artifacts, and
production release as part of the security

26
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:08.704
boundary.

27
00:01:08.784 --> 00:01:12.296
The goal is firmware whose assumptions are
safe, bounded, documented, tested,

28
00:01:12.376 --> 00:01:14.420
and reviewable across the product
lifecycle.
